ix}3x_{1} \\\\ x_{1} + 2y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} + 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}; = T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation." 13194139534680 false] [17592186087497 1145 "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1.$ Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2}xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}k{x}^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne k{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2.$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3kx \\\\ kx+2ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(3x) \\\\ k(x+2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+2{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{y}_{1}\\e